The problem is real and I could not refute it. The callout's only citation, perry1952, is a 1952 edition of Greek and Latin texts, so it cannot support anything about present-day India. "One of India's best-known children's stories" is a claim of scale with no number or source, which STYLE_GUIDE §7 rules out ("Claims of scale carry numbers with sources or don't appear"). The lesson's own Pass C flag ("rests on the research file's evidence ..., not on Perry — confirm, or relabel it as a usage FACT") and research-notes open item 3 already concede both points.

The diagnosis and the new sentence are sound: take the sentence out of the callout, drop the superlative, and say "at least one" reader. The fix set fails as written in three ways.
(1) Fix 1 adds [REF:paton1917 9.272], [REF:rackham1940 10.125] and [REF:duff1934 Avianus 27]. None of these keys is in REFERENCES.md, and this finding proposes no entries for them. rackham1940 cites Pliny, who does not appear in the callout; the lesson says his sentence "is **not used**". "Avianus 27" is a fable number that the lesson's flag and open item 4 record as unverified. These belong to other items, not to this one.
(2) Fix 2 cites piyusham5, but no fix adds that key to REFERENCES.md. Applied as written, the fixes produce 5 validator errors (the original produces 0).
(3) The fix set is incomplete. The same superlative stays in objective 4 (en: "today one of India's best-known children's stories"; hi: "भारत की सबसे जानी-मानी बाल-कथाओं में से एक") and in two §26 reel captions ("India's favourite crow story"; "Today: one of India's best-known children's stories."). Objective 4 would then ask learners to state a claim the lesson no longer teaches.

The corrected fixes:
- leave the callout with its original [REF:perry1952 no. 390] only, re-wrapped;
- add the plain-prose sentence with IAST for the two Sanskrit items, following the STYLE_GUIDE §1 layer order and the lesson's own practice (the validator's transliteration check passes);
- add a piyusham5 entry to REFERENCES.md and a matching site label in scripts/build_site.py;
- align objective 4 (en and hi) with the §20 revision box and quiz Q8(b), and the Hindi with §7's own phrase;
- remove the superlatives from the reel captions.

Not fixed, for the editor to consider: §4's intro line "almost everyone can tell it" is also a scale claim, and l05 §21 has "loved across India". After the edits, the JSON export needs regenerating with scripts/export_json.py.

I tried to refute this and could not. The problem is real by the course's own standard, and the proposed fix is accurate and properly hedged. It needs one small change.

My best counter-argument was the dictionary sense of "fake", which is just "not genuine". In that sense the word does fit the Mahābhārata attribution: at all five places the epic's verse goes on with something else. But the hook names no source. It says "this famous Sanskrit line" has a fake second half, which claims the half-line is spurious everywhere. That is a claim that it was made up and that no genuine source exists anywhere. The evidence supports only two things: the line is absent from the critical edition, and no source has been found (C5).

The searches were large but not complete. Several Purāṇas could not be fully searched (C7), and the critical edition's variant readings are not in the e-text. Also, the opening अहिंसा परमो धर्मः recurs across Sanskrit texts with many different genuine second halves. So the exact claim is "no source found", and the course itself chose that wording in four places:
- the lesson's Metadata, verification flag (2): "phrased 'no source has been found', with no claim about origin or date";
- the Teacher Notes: "the course claims none of that";
- the §24 video caption: "Not in the critical edition. No source found.";
- this reel's own payoff: "the source line is as sure as the evidence".

C7 rejects "never scripture" on the same grounds.

The proposed wording claims nothing about origin and matches C5's verified "no source has been found". Its one flaw is that in a cold-open hook "the epic" points to nothing: the poster carries no attribution, and the epic is first named in the demo. Naming the Mahābhārata fixes that and leads straight into the demo's locus chips.
